🚨 **BLOCKING: `_extractDiffFromObject` carries verdicts off identical degraded reads — the `'diff --git'` guard is missing on the envelope paths**

`_extractDiffFromString` correctly rejects plain strings that don't contain `diff --git` (line 355), but both envelope paths skip that check:

- JSON-string envelope (line 353): `_extractDiffFromObject(JSON.parse(raw))` → returns the unwrapped value unchecked.
- Object envelope (lines 358–366): `if (typeof v === 'string' && v.length) return v;` accepts **any** non-empty string under `diff` / `result` / `arg$1` / `body`.

Since `guestPrDiffText` calls both diff tools within milliseconds of each other, an upstream failure (proxy page, rate-limit notice, 502 body) surfaces as the **same** envelope for the source PR and the transplant PR. Reproduced against this branch:

```bash
node -e "
var mod = require('./js/common/guestTransplant.js');
var cfg = { carryLabels: ['ai_pr_reviewed', 'pr_approved'] };
var srcErr = JSON.stringify({ result: 'GitHub API is down (502)' });
console.log(mod.carryDecision(srcErr, srcErr, cfg, ['po_accept', 'ai_pr_reviewed', 'pr_approved']));
"
# → {"carry":true,"labels":["ai_pr_reviewed","pr_approved"],"reason":"identical-diff"}
```

`pr_approved` gets stamped onto the unreviewed transplant PR from two identical error pages — `merge-validated-guest` can then merge it without any diff ever being compared. This violates the module's own invariant (`guestTransplant.js:394`: "every degraded read is a fresh pipeline") and the AC6 premise that verdicts carry only on a byte-identical *diff*.

Fix — apply the same containment check to the unwrapped value:

```js
function _extractDiffFromObject(obj) {
    if (!obj || typeof obj !== 'object') return null;
    var keys = ['diff', 'result', 'arg$1', 'body'];
    for (var i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
        var v = obj[keys[i]];
        if (typeof v === 'string' && v.indexOf('diff --git') !== -1) return v;
    }
    return null;
}
```

Two related notes (no separate threads): nested envelopes (`{"result":{"diff":...}}`) currently return `null` — fail-closed to a fresh pipeline, acceptable; and please add a unit test pining the negative case (`carryDecision` on identical non-diff envelopes must NOT carry) so this can't regress.

⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the latch exemption trusts an attacker-controllable branch name — no same-repo/machine check**

`_isGuestTransplantItem` classifies purely on `parseGuestBranch(item.branch, 'ai/guest-')`, but `item.branch` is `(p.head && p.head.ref) || p.headRefName` (`githubSource.js:1060` `_prItemFromPr` → `_prListBranch`) — i.e. the **fork's** branch name for cross-repo PRs. An external fork can name its branch `ai/guest-42`, and once that PR is green + CLEAN + zero-lifecycle-labels it now matches `validate-fresh-masked-green` **without** the `ai_developed` latch that gh-829 defines as the machine-work gate (rule description: "a guest/unlinked PR without the latch stays excluded exactly as before, AC3").

Impact today is bounded — the branch-targeted `validate_pr` lane is fork-blind (no-ref dispatch → no `ai_validating` arm, `smAgent.js:939–955`), and the merge path still requires the sticky `pr_approved` gate — so the spoof yields repeated failed dispatch attempts and log noise per tick, not code execution. But it deliberately relaxes a fail-closed security gate on an unverified string; tightening it is one line, since `headRepo` is already on the item:

```js
function _isGuestTransplantItem(item, repoInfo) {
    var headFull = item.headRepo || (item.pr && item.pr.headRepo) || null;
    if (headFull && headFull !== repoInfo.owner + '/' + repoInfo.repo) return false;
    var b = String((item && item.branch) || (item && item.pr && item.pr.branch) || '');
    return guestTransplantModule.parseGuestBranch(b, guestTransplantModule.DEFAULTS.branchPrefix) !== null;
}
```

(`repoInfo` is already a parameter of `_mergeStateLatchGuardFail` — thread it through.) Please also pin the spoof case with a unit test: a fork PR (`head.ref: 'ai/guest-999'`, `headRepo: 'attacker/repo'`) must still be excluded by the latch.

